## std::async_fs — async file I/O.
##
## Spec: docs/specs/stdlib/async_io.spec.md Milestone 4B (B4–B6).
##
## Three hand-written futures backed by the per-thread reactor in
## library/std/future/runtime/reactor.c (sub-phase 4A). The futures
## reuse the same pattern as TimeSleepFuture: an `Int` reactor handle
## stashed on the future, registered lazily on the first poll that
## hits EAGAIN, deregistered in `def drop`.
##
## Surface (mirrors the spec):
##
##   AsyncFile.open(path)         -> AsyncOpenFuture (read-only)
##   AsyncFile.create(path)       -> AsyncOpenFuture (write+create+trunc)
##   f.read_to_string()           -> AsyncReadToStringFuture
##   f.write_all(content)         -> AsyncWriteAllFuture
##   f.close                      -> Result[(), IoError]   (sync convenience)
##
## Why a separate `async_fs` package rather than extending `fs/src/lib.rx`:
##   - `fs/src/lib.rx` loads BEFORE `future/src/lib.rx` in
##     BOOTSTRAP_FILES (see `compiler/ruxen_core/src/resolve/bootstrap.rs`),
##     so `include Future` would fail to resolve at registration time
##     if AsyncFile / the three futures lived in fs.rx.
##   - The package boundary also gives 4C's AsyncTcpStream / Listener a
##     parallel home (`library/std/async_net`) without forcing async
##     I/O into the synchronous fs/net packages.

## --- Reactor fd-readiness primitives (sub-phase 4B reactor extension)
##
## Live in the same C TU as the timer primitives because they share the
## per-thread reactor singleton + slot table. Declared here (rather
## than in `library/std/future/src/lib.rx` alongside the timer decls)
## so we don't churn the future package — async_fs is the only caller
## until 4C also pulls them in.
lib "../../future/runtime/reactor.c"
  def ruxen_reactor_register_fd_read(reactor: Int, fd: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_reactor_register_fd_write(reactor: Int, fd: Int) -> Int
end

## --- async_fs state-machine primitives (Int-only signatures) -------
##
## See library/std/async_fs/runtime/async_fs.c for the C side. The
## read/write step machines own a heap state struct; the Ruxen futures
## hold its address as an Int and dispatch through these free fns.
##
## Declared here (rather than after AsyncFile) because every type
## referenced is already in scope from core/io/future — no forward
## reference. The `ruxen_async_file_open` / `_fd` decls that reference
## AsyncFile live in a second lib block below the class.
lib "runtime/async_fs.c"
  def ruxen_async_read_state_new(fd: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_async_read_state_get_fd(state: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_async_read_step(state: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_async_read_state_take_result(state: Int) -> Result[String, IoError]
  def ruxen_async_read_state_free(state: Int) -> ()

  def ruxen_async_write_state_new(fd: Int, content: &String) -> Int
  def ruxen_async_write_state_get_fd(state: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_async_write_step(state: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_async_write_state_take_result(state: Int) -> Result[(), IoError]
  def ruxen_async_write_state_free(state: Int) -> ()
end

## Workaround for the 4B e2e fixture: the canonical `String == String`
## codegen path (cranelift/emit.rs `is_string_typed_value`) currently
## misses the MIR shape produced by destructuring `Ok(s)` out of a
## `Result[String, IoError]` returned via the manual poll loop — the
## Local::ty for `s` ends up not matching `Ty::String`, so the
## generated Compare is integer-pointer-eq rather than `ruxen_string_eq`.
## Routing through this typed lib decl gives the fixture deterministic
## value-equality without depending on whichever path typeck picks for
## `==`. v1 follow-up: thread the destructured-payload ty through the
## Compare lowering so `==` works on String for every MIR shape.
lib "../string/runtime/string.c"
  def ruxen_async_str_equals as "ruxen_string_eq"(a: &String, b: &String) -> Int
end
